<div class="container">

<header class="main-header">
  <div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos &middot; Orach Chaim</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Rosh HaShanah</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Halacha 1</div>
  <div class="halacha-num">Inyan Shofar &middot; 5 Oaseyos + Inyan HaMai&rsquo;ah Kolos</div>
  <div class="source-ref-header">Based on what Rabbainu, of blessed memory, wrote<br>in the ma&rsquo;amar &ldquo;T&rsquo;homos Y&rsquo;chasyumu&rdquo; (siman 9)<br>on the passuk: &ldquo;A window [<span class="heb-term">tzohar</span>] shall you make for the <span class="heb-term">taivah</span>,&rdquo; etc.,<br>&ldquo;and the opening of the <span class="heb-term">taivah</span> you shall place on its side,&rdquo; etc.,<br>&ldquo;lower, second, and third [stories],&rdquo; etc.</div>
</header>

<div class="preamble">
And the general rule is: when the darkness and the <span class="heb-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> [husks/shells] surround from every side, the main <span class="heb-term">tikkun</span> [rectification] is through <span class="concept">emes</span> [truth] &mdash; that you should see that the <span class="heb-term">dibbur</span> [speech] of the <span class="heb-term">t&rsquo;fillah</span> [prayer] goes out from your mouth in truth. And through this, Hashem, blessed be He, will illuminate for him in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;Hashem ori v&rsquo;yish&rsquo;i&rdquo;</span> [&ldquo;Hashem is my light and my salvation&rdquo;] <span class="verse-source">(T&rsquo;hillim 27:1)</span>. And he will merit to make an opening in the darkness in which he is trapped. See there well. And more than what is written there, I heard orally [<span class="heb-term">peh el peh</span>] from his holy mouth. And through this, the <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">tikkun</span> of t&rsquo;fillah</span> is accomplished, which is the aspect of Yaakov and his sons. See there well.
</div>

<!-- OAS 1 -->
<div class="section-marker">1</div>

<p class="no-indent">And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">kol [sound/voice] of the Shofar</span>, which hints at t&rsquo;shuvah [repentance], in the aspect of &ldquo;improve [<span class="heb-term">shap&rsquo;ru</span>] your deeds.&rdquo; As is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Awake, sleepers, from your slumber!&rdquo;</span> And therefore the Shofar, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Alma D&rsquo;Asai</span> [the World to Come], is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Binah</span> [Understanding], the aspect of t&rsquo;shuvah, as is known. And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">kol that goes out from the Shofar</span>, which is the aspect of a <span class="concept">kol of emes</span> [voice of truth] that goes out from the depths of the heart [<span class="heb-term">umka d&rsquo;libba</span>], in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;from the depths I called to You, Hashem&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(T&rsquo;hillim 130:1)</span>, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;from the narrow place I called to Y-ah&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(T&rsquo;hillim 118:5)</span>. Meaning, due to the great intensification of the one who surrounds from every side, then they turn themselves to the truth and call to Hashem in truth from the depths of the heart &mdash; through which one is saved from the darkness, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;and the opening of the <span class="heb-term">taivah</span> you shall place on its side,&rdquo;</span> as above.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;He answered me in the expanse, Y-ah&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(T&rsquo;hillim 118:5)</span> &mdash; that one merits to be broadened and to go out, as above. For the kol of the Shofar is the aspect of <span class="concept">emes</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="concept">Yaakov</span>, as is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Give emes to Yaakov&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Michah 7:20)</span>, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;the kol is the kol of Yaakov&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(B&rsquo;raishis 27:22)</span>. And this is what Rabbainu wrote there: that <span class="concept">Yaakov is the aspect of t&rsquo;fillah</span>, for the main <span class="heb-term">tikkun</span> of t&rsquo;fillah is through emes, as above, which is the aspect of Yaakov, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore in the Torah only the <span class="concept">T&rsquo;ruah</span> is mentioned, and the other kolos [sounds] we learn from there, as Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said. For the T&rsquo;ruah is the aspect of Yaakov &mdash; the kol of the Shofar fundamentally hints at him, as above. And therefore the <span class="concept">T&rsquo;ruah is <span class="heb-term">g&rsquo;nuchi ganach</span></span> [groaning groans] <span class="concept">and <span class="heb-term">y&rsquo;lulai yalil</span></span> [wailing wails] &mdash; meaning, that which he groans and wails and calls to Hashem to return to Him, as above. And therefore every T&rsquo;ruah has a <span class="concept">P&rsquo;shutah [straight blast] before it and a P&rsquo;shutah after it</span> &mdash; this also hints at t&rsquo;shuvah: at first, he was <span class="heb-term">pashut</span> [straight/simple] and rectified &mdash; meaning, before the corruption. And after he was corrupted and broken, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;disgrace has broken my heart&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(T&rsquo;hillim 69:21)</span>, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;and they pursued them until the &lsquo;breakings&rsquo;&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Y&rsquo;hoshua 7:5)</span> &mdash; then through the T&rsquo;ruah, which is the aspect of emes as above, through this he returns and becomes straightened [<span class="heb-term">nifshatt</span>]. And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">T&rsquo;kiah after it</span>, which is a straight [<span class="heb-term">pashut</span>] sound.</p>

<p>And therefore we blow [the Shofar] on <span class="concept">Rosh HaShanah</span>, for then is the intensification of the <span class="heb-term">dinim</span> [judgments], which are the aspect of the darkness. And therefore one needs to draw himself to the emes, which is the aspect of the kol of the Shofar, as above. And it is a <span class="concept">remembrance for the first day</span> [<span class="heb-term">zikaron l&rsquo;yom rishon</span>] &mdash; for then, at the beginning of the <span class="heb-term">B&rsquo;riyah</span> [Creation], the darkness intensified, covering the face of the deep [<span class="heb-term">t&rsquo;hom</span>]. And the <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">Ru&rsquo;ach Elokim</span></span> [Spirit of G-d], which is the aspect of Yaakov &mdash; who is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ru&rsquo;ach</span> [spirit], as is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;and the spirit of Yaakov their father was revived&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(B&rsquo;raishis 45:27)</span> &mdash; who is the aspect of emes, as above &mdash; was hovering over the face of the waters, to drive out and clear the darkness through the aspect of emes. And through this, the light was revealed, as is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;and Elokim said, &lsquo;Let there be light,&rsquo; and there was light&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(B&rsquo;raishis 1:3)</span>. As is explained above, that through emes, the light of Hashem, blessed be He, shines &mdash; in the aspect of &ldquo;Hashem ori&rdquo; [&ldquo;Hashem is my light&rdquo;] &mdash; as is explained there, well. See there. And through this, the <span class="heb-term">B&rsquo;riyah</span> came into being.</p>

<p>And therefore <span class="concept">emes is the sustenance of the world</span>, for the main creation [<span class="heb-term">b&rsquo;riyah</span>] was through emes, as above. And this is what Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said on the passuk <span class="verse">&ldquo;bor&rsquo;acha Yaakov&rdquo;</span> [&ldquo;your Creator, Yaakov&rdquo;]: &ldquo;My world, my world! Who created you? Yaakov created you!&rdquo; etc. For the main creation of the world was through the aspect of Yaakov, which is the aspect of emes, as above.</p>

<p>And this is what Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said: <span class="concept">&ldquo;T&rsquo;shuvah preceded the world&rdquo;</span> [<span class="heb-term">T&rsquo;shuvah kadmah la&rsquo;olam</span>] &mdash; for the main t&rsquo;shuvah is through emes, by which one drives out the darkness and makes an opening to go out, as above &mdash; and this aspect preceded the world. And through this, the creation of the world came into being &mdash; in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;and darkness was on the face of the deep, and the Spirit of G-d was hovering,&rdquo;</span> etc., <span class="verse">&ldquo;and there was light,&rdquo;</span> as above.</p>

<p>And therefore <span class="concept">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is a remembrance for the first day &mdash; and therefore then begin the <span class="concept">days of t&rsquo;shuvah</span>. And therefore we blow then with the Shofar, which is the aspect of a kol of emes that makes an opening and broadens, to go out, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore the <span class="concept">Shofar is narrow from above and wide from below</span>. And one needs to blow specifically in the narrow place [<span class="heb-term">makom ha&rsquo;katzar</span>]. And if he blew in the wide place, he has not fulfilled [his obligation]. For one who wishes to enter must enter specifically through the narrow opening &mdash; in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;and the opening of the <span class="heb-term">taivah</span> you shall place on its side.&rdquo;</span> As I heard from Rabbainu, of blessed memory, orally, in the explanation that one needs to make a <span class="concept">new opening</span> specifically. And as Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said: the letter <span class="heb-term">Hai</span> [ה] resembles this world, etc., and whoever wishes to leave [may leave], etc., and from the side it is open &mdash; whoever wishes to enter may enter. And they ask there: to enter through the same [opening] as one who exits? And they answer there: it is not sufficient, etc. See there. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;and the opening of the <span class="heb-term">taivah</span> you shall place on its side&rdquo;</span> &mdash; &ldquo;on its side&rdquo; specifically, for one needs to enter specifically through the narrow opening that is from the side &mdash; meaning, from the side of the <span class="heb-term">Hai</span>, as above. And then Hashem, blessed be He, widens the opening for him and brings him out. And then <span class="verse">&ldquo;He answered me in the expanse, Y-ah,&rdquo;</span> as above. And this is the aspect of the Shofar, which is narrow from above, etc., and one needs to blow and bring out the kol of the Shofar &mdash; which is a kol of emes &mdash; specifically from the narrow place, and then Hashem, blessed be He, widens for him, as above.</p>

<!-- OAS 2 -->
<div class="section-marker">2</div>

<p class="no-indent">And therefore, also, if one <span class="concept">turned the Shofar inside out</span> and blew it, he has not fulfilled [his obligation] &mdash; whether he turned it as one turns a garment [<span class="heb-term">chaluk</span>], etc., or whether he widened it, etc. For the main t&rsquo;shuvah as it should be is that one should do the t&rsquo;shuvah <span class="concept">in that very body itself</span>, and in that very matter itself in which he sinned &mdash; and should return and go back through the narrow and pressed places, to Hashem, blessed be He. But if his body is turned over and changed, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;it is turned like clay under a seal, and they stand as though [re]clothed&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Iyov 38:14)</span> &mdash; that, <span class="heb-term">chas v&rsquo;shalom</span>, his body should be overturned and changed &mdash; meaning, that he should die and return and be reincarnated [<span class="heb-term">yisgalgail</span>], and afterward return and cry out to Hashem &mdash; this is not proper t&rsquo;shuvah. For one needs to return <span class="concept">in this very body</span> [<span class="heb-term">ba&rsquo;guf ha&rsquo;zeh</span>] specifically, as he is, and go through the narrow and pressed place, and from there call out and return to Hashem in emes. Then Hashem, blessed be He, will widen for him &mdash; and then it is the aspect of <span class="concept">blowing the Shofar properly</span> [<span class="heb-term">k&rsquo;hilchaso</span>] &mdash; meaning, the aspect of t&rsquo;shuvah in emes as is fitting, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore if [the Shofar] <span class="concept">has a hole</span> [<span class="heb-term">nikav</span>], the <span class="concept">sealing must be of its own kind</span> [<span class="heb-term">b&rsquo;mino</span>] specifically. For in that very matter in which he sinned, he should rectify &mdash; as Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said, and as above. (And as I heard in clear explanation from his holy mouth, these very words.)</p>

<!-- OAS 3 -->
<div class="section-marker">3</div>

<p class="no-indent">And therefore a <span class="concept">yachid [individual] can fulfill the obligation of the rabbim [public]</span> in the blowing of the Shofar. For the main thing depends on the <span class="concept">yachid who awakens and cries out in emes to Hashem</span>, blessed be He &mdash; through which he makes an opening through which others can go out from their darkness. As I heard from Rabbainu, of blessed memory, on <span class="verse">&ldquo;and the opening of the <span class="heb-term">taivah</span> you shall place on its side&rdquo;</span> &mdash; see there, in another place, on this explanation. And since the yachid intends to bring others out from their darkness, then whoever wishes to go out and directs his heart to this, he can go out through the opening that the yachid made, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore the blowing of the Shofar depends on <span class="concept">kavanas ha&rsquo;laiv</span> [intention of the heart] &mdash; and the one who blows as if merely occupied [<span class="heb-term">k&rsquo;mis&rsquo;asaik</span>] or to make music, he has not fulfilled [his obligation]. For after the intention of the heart are the matters &mdash; for one needs to direct his heart greatly and bring out the kol in emes from the depths of the heart, as above.</p>

<p>And this is what Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said: <span class="concept">&ldquo;Whoever is not obligated in a matter cannot fulfill the obligation of the public.&rdquo;</span> In this is hinted: specifically the one who is very obligated in his own eyes &mdash; or who is truly obligated &mdash; and then, from the greatness of the darkness and the pressure and the crowding that surrounds him from all sides, he cries out in emes to Hashem, blessed be He &mdash; this one can make an opening through which others can also go out. And so Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said: <span class="concept">&ldquo;We do not appoint a <span class="heb-term">parnas</span> [leader] over the community unless a basket of creeping things [<span class="heb-term">kuppah shel sh&rsquo;ratzim</span>] hangs behind him,&rdquo;</span> etc. And therefore specifically the one who is obligated in the matter of t&rsquo;shuvah and upon whom the yoke of t&rsquo;shuvah rests &mdash; this one can fulfill the obligation of others, to make for them also an opening to go out &mdash; through his calling in emes to Hashem, blessed be He.</p>

<!-- OAS 4 -->
<div class="section-marker">4</div>

<p class="no-indent">And therefore the <span class="concept">T&rsquo;ruah is tripled</span> [<span class="heb-term">m&rsquo;shuleshes</span>], and so the totality of the blowing of the Shofar is <span class="concept">tripled &mdash; three of three of three</span> [<span class="heb-term">shalosh shel shalosh shalosh</span>]. For the main blowing of the Shofar is the aspect of <span class="concept">emes, which is tripled</span> [<span class="heb-term">m&rsquo;shulash</span>] &mdash; in the aspect of fire, water, wind [<span class="heb-term">aish, mayim, ru&rsquo;ach</span>] &mdash; in the aspect of beginning, middle, end [<span class="heb-term">rosh, soch, sof</span>]. And through it, one rectifies the <span class="concept">three worlds</span> &mdash; in the aspect of &ldquo;lower, second, and third [stories]&rdquo; [<span class="heb-term">tachtiyim, sh&rsquo;niyim, u&rsquo;sh&rsquo;lishim</span>] (as Rabbainu wrote there; see there).</p>

<p>And therefore the <span class="concept">T&rsquo;kiyos are during the T&rsquo;fillah</span> [prayer], for through them is the <span class="heb-term">tikkun</span> of the T&rsquo;fillah, as above. And therefore we blow <span class="concept">twelve T&rsquo;ruos</span> &mdash; meaning, six sitting [<span class="heb-term">m&rsquo;yushav</span>] and six standing [<span class="heb-term">m&rsquo;umad</span>] &mdash; for in the thirty kolos of <span class="heb-term">m&rsquo;yushav</span> there are six T&rsquo;ruos, and so in the thirty kolos that we blow according to the order of the <span class="heb-term">B&rsquo;rachos</span> [blessings] &mdash; for these are the main obligation [<span class="heb-term">chiyuv</span>], and the rest are additional &mdash; corresponding to the <span class="concept">twelve Sh&rsquo;vatim of the sons of Yaakov</span>, which are the aspect of the <span class="concept">twelve <span class="heb-term">nuschaos</span> [versions] of t&rsquo;fillah</span> that are rectified through emes, which is the aspect of the T&rsquo;ruah, as above (and as is written there; see there).</p>

<!-- OAS 5 -->
<div class="section-marker">5</div>

<p class="no-indent">And therefore the <span class="concept">Shofar is the aspect of Y&rsquo;tzias Mitzrayim</span> [the Exodus from Egypt], as is known. For the main exile of Mitzrayim was through the <span class="concept">damage to t&rsquo;fillah</span> [<span class="heb-term">p&rsquo;gam ha&rsquo;t&rsquo;fillah</span>] (as is explained there). And so too in the future [<span class="heb-term">l&rsquo;asid</span>] it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;and a great Shofar will be blown&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Y&rsquo;shayahu 27:13)</span> &mdash; for the main <span class="concept">G&rsquo;ulah</span> [Redemption] depends on this &mdash; through the t&rsquo;fillah being rectified through the aspect of emes, as above (as is explained there; see there).</p>

<p>The Shofar is the aspect of Yaakov, in the aspect of <span class="concept">&ldquo;shufreh d&rsquo;Yaakov mai&rsquo;ain shufreh d&rsquo;Adam&rdquo;</span> [&ldquo;the beauty of Yaakov was a semblance of the beauty of Adam&rdquo;]. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;Blow the Shofar &hellip; and raise a banner&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Y&rsquo;shayahu 27:13)</span>. For through the Shofar, which is the aspect of emes and the <span class="heb-term">tikkun</span> of t&rsquo;fillah &mdash; through this, the <span class="concept">miracles [<span class="heb-term">nisim</span>] are elevated</span>, which is the aspect of t&rsquo;fillah, as is explained there.</p>

<!-- INYAN HA'MAI'AH KOLOS -->
<div class="sub-header">Inyan HaMai&rsquo;ah Kolos<br><span style="font-size:15px;font-weight:400;color:var(--text-light)">[The Matter of the One Hundred Sounds]</span></div>

<p class="no-indent">Based on what Rabbainu said in the ma&rsquo;amar <span class="concept">&ldquo;Pasach Rabbi Shim&rsquo;on: Ais La&rsquo;asos La&rsquo;Shem&rdquo;</span> (siman 60) &mdash; that the Shofar is the aspect of the <span class="concept">revelation of <span class="heb-term">yir&rsquo;ah</span></span> [fear/awe], through which one merits <span class="concept">great wealth</span> [<span class="heb-term">ashirus g&rsquo;dolah</span>]. And the <span class="heb-term">yir&rsquo;ah</span> is the aspect of <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">Pachad Yitzchak</span></span> [the Fear of Yitzchak]. And Yitzchak merited wealth through drawing <span class="heb-term">arichas yamim</span> [length of days], the aspect of <span class="heb-term">yir&rsquo;ah</span>, in the land of the P&rsquo;lishtim, as is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;and it was, when the days were long for him there,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="verse-source">(B&rsquo;raishis 26:8)</span>. And through this he merited wealth, as is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;and Yitzchak sowed &hellip; and he found <span class="concept">a hundred gates</span> [<span class="heb-term">mai&rsquo;ah sh&rsquo;arim</span>]&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(B&rsquo;raishis 26:12)</span>.</p>

<p>And corresponding to this, we blow <span class="concept">one hundred kolos</span> &mdash; corresponding to the <span class="heb-term">mai&rsquo;ah sh&rsquo;arim</span> that one merits through the Shofar, as above. And therefore the blowing of the Shofar is <span class="concept">three of three of three</span> &mdash; the aspect of the revelation of the completeness of <span class="heb-term">yir&rsquo;ah</span>, which is <span class="concept">three lines [<span class="heb-term">kavim</span>]</span>, and each line includes three (as is explained there).</p>

<p>And therefore one needs to <span class="concept">call out [<span class="heb-term">likros</span>] before the one who blows</span> [<span class="heb-term">tokei&rsquo;a</span>]. For the main revelation of <span class="heb-term">yir&rsquo;ah</span> is through <span class="heb-term">dibbur</span> [speech] that goes out through the awakening of sleep [<span class="heb-term">his&rsquo;or&rsquo;rus ha&rsquo;shainah</span>] (as is explained there). And therefore there is an opinion that for the <span class="concept">first T&rsquo;kiah</span> one should not call out before the one who blows &mdash; for then it is still the aspect of the <span class="concept">absence of speech</span> [<span class="heb-term">he&rsquo;dayr ha&rsquo;dibbur</span>], in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;you have struck [<span class="heb-term">takasta</span>] your palm to a stranger,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="verse-source">(Mishlai 6:1)</span> &mdash; as Rabbainu wrote there that the T&rsquo;kiah hints at this.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">reddening of the face of the one who blows</span> [<span class="heb-term">his&rsquo;admus p&rsquo;nai ha&rsquo;tokei&rsquo;a</span>] &mdash; meaning, through the seventy faces [<span class="heb-term">shiv&rsquo;im panim</span>] of the awakening of sleep (as is explained there). And therefore the main blowing of the Shofar is for the <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">tikkun</span> of the <span class="heb-term">B&rsquo;ris</span></span> [covenant] (as is found in the <span class="heb-term">K&rsquo;savim</span> [writings of the Arizal]). For through the <span class="heb-term">yir&rsquo;ah</span> that is revealed through the Shofar, the beauty of women [<span class="heb-term">y&rsquo;fi ha&rsquo;nashim</span>] is subdued, and one merits the <span class="heb-term">tikkun</span> of the <span class="heb-term">B&rsquo;ris</span>, as is explained there.</p>

